{"kind":"artifact","text":"README companion at exact clean head 6da2898ae6a9e985dd6cc3d180daa6d6fc7939f4 on request/serve-drop-acknowledge-flag, cut from main 0ec3618f.\n\nThe quickstart invocation drops the --acknowledge-trusted-processes argument, and the paragraph that explained why the acknowledgement was deliberate is replaced by a direct statement of the boundary: this is a single-operator local service, running it is the decision to accept its boundary — trusted processes only, every process inside this resident boundary can act as every actor key this application can open — and the service prints that sentence next to its address on every start.\n\nThis is the first page a new reader meets, so it is the one where losing the disclosure would have cost most. Before this head the string \"trusted processes only\" appeared nowhere outside the constant at internal/service/server.go:39; it is now in README.md and in the four documentation pages covered by the companion docs artifact.\n\nOne consequence a reader of this page should know, recorded in assert cead45a5 rather than in the README: the removal is not backward compatible for a launch script that still passes the flag. Go's flag package refuses an unknown flag, so an existing invocation carrying it will fail to start after the rebuild. The fix is to drop the argument, which is what this page now shows.\n\nGates at this exact head, after go clean -testcache: make vet, make build, make docs, make test and make race all exit 0, 28 packages ok, zero failures, zero races; gofmt -l . empty; git status --porcelain empty; grep for the flag name returns nothing anywhere in the worktree.\n\nThis artifact covers README.md. Companion artifacts at cmd/gs and docs stand at this same head and must be co-signed with it.","body":{"commit":"6da2898ae6a9e985dd6cc3d180daa6d6fc7939f4","path":"README.md"}}